messages.body_plain:
Hi,

Is it possible to make predictions using mixture model?  Assuming normal mixture model below
what would be the generated quantities block to calculate predictive posterior? What would be the meaning of 95% credible interval of this predictive posterior?

Thank you,
Linas

data {
  int<lower=1> K; // number of mixture components
  int<lower=1> N; // number of data points
  real y[N]; // observations
}
parameters {
  simplex[K] theta; // mixing proportions
  real mu[K]; // locations of mixture components
  real<lower=0> sigma[K]; // scales of mixture components
}
model {
  real ps[K]; // temp for log component densities
  sigma ~ cauchy(0,2.5);
  mu ~ normal(0,10);
  for (n in 1:N) {
    for (k in 1:K) {
      ps[k] <- log(theta[k]) + normal_log(y[n],mu[k],sigma[k]);
    }
    increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(ps));
  }
}

I've looked at a few previous examples, and I'm still not understanding why Stan is throwing an error. Hopfully, it is just obtuseness on my part.

I have a dataset which includes a matrix (dataframe) of 10 columns of 100 numbers (fake losses) each. The idea is that each column is a new year, and for each column, there are 100 rows representing 100 observations. I am trying to build a simple model to see if I can recover the underlying trend in fake data I have created. If that works, I can start playing with real data.

As I am not interested in using matrix algebra, my understanding of the documentation is that the most efficient way to code this data would be as an array of length 10 of column vectors, each of length 100. I could then loop through the columns and take advantage of vectorized calls. I know my data is in the form of a 100 x 10 matrix (calls to dim show that), but when I run Stan, I get the error:

Error : mismatch in dimension declared and found in context; processing stage=data initialization; variable name=Losses; position=0; dims declared=(10,100); dims found=(100,10)

The code to build the raw data is:

set.seed(68951)
library(dplyr)
library(reshape2)
library(rstan)
trend <- .05
theta <- 1e6
alpha <- 5
ScaleTrendFactor <- (1 + trend) ^ seq_len(10)
IndLoss <- rgamma(1000, shape = 5, scale = theta * rep(ScaleTrendFactor, each = 100))
Years <- rep(seq_len(10), each = 100)
Obs <- rep(seq_len(100), 10)
LossData <- tbl_df(data.frame(Years, Obs, IndLoss))
names(LossData) <- c('Years', 'Obs', 'Losses')
DAT <- list(N = max(LossData$Years), Obs = max(LossData$Obs), Losses = acast(LossData, Obs ~ Years, value.var = 'Losses'))

Just to ensure:

> dim(DAT$Losses)
 [1] 100 10

The stan code (stored in a file called "Gamma_Bayes.stan")  is:

data {
  int<lower = 1> N;
  int<lower = 1> Obs;
  vector[Obs] Losses[N];
}
parameters {
  real<lower = 0> shape;
  real<lower = 0> trend;
  real<lower = 0> rate;
}
transformed parameters{
  real scale;
  scale <- 1 / rate;
}
model{
  vector[Obs] L;
  rate ~ normal(0.1, .1);
  trend ~ uniform(0.0, 0.25);
  shape ~ uniform(0.0, 10.0);

  for (i in 1:N){
    Losses[i] ~ gamma(shape, rate / pow(1.0 + trend, i));
  }
}

The call to stan is:

A <- stan(file = 'Gamma_Bayes.stan', data = DAT, iter = 20000, chains = 5, par = c('shape', 'rate', 'scale', 'trend'))

When I switch the order of N (10) and Obs(100) in defining the "Losses" array, Stan runs, but returns rediculous responses with neff = 2 and Rhats in the 1e6 zone. The same issues hold if I define it as a matrix.

What am I missing (and feel free to trout me with the entire printed manual if appropriate 8-) ).

Thank you.

Hi Stan Users Group,

Apologies if this type of question has been covered directly elsewhere; I have looked through relevant posts here <https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/JI6yrSZz_Xg/-MGIHytSCSMJ>, here <https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/xv-Yjpb7faw/O9cydg8NI8sJ> and here <https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/Ur7YPqUoUYM/0uYWTdeIRpUJ> and also the manual under problematic posteriors. I've tried a few things but I'm still struggling to solve my problem.

I am trying to fit a three parameter nonlinear function to some data. I want to estimate how each of these parameters changes across a number of experimental conditions. To do this I thought to use a design matrix composed of sum-to-zero contrasts (created using Python equiv of r's contr.sum()). The beta weights of this design matrix are the offsets to the parameters caused by the conditions, and the intercept is the average parameter value across all the data.

The problem comes in because the three parameters for the main model have bounds: specifically, all must be positive, and one also has an upper bound.

Example: parameter alpha is bounded between 0 and 5 (these constraints are determined by the problem setting). Here's a sketch of the relevant model components for alpha:

parameters {
  real<lower=0, upper=5> alpha;
  vector<lower=-5, upper=5>[D-1] alpha_offsets_raw;

...

transformed parameters {
    vector<lower=-3, upper=3>[D] alpha_offsets;
    # constrain offsets to sum to zero:
    for (i in 1:(D-1)) {
        alpha_offsets[i] <- alpha_offsets_raw[i];
    }
    alpha_offsets[D] <- -sum(alpha_offsets_raw);
...

model {
    alpha ~ normal(3, 1);
    alpha_offsets_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

    ...

    # loop over data rows:
    for (i in 1:N) {
        real this_alpha;
        this_alpha <- alpha + dot_product(X[i], alpha_offsets);
        ...
        y[i] ~ some_function(z[i], this_alpha, ...)
}

X is a design matrix (actually, N vectors of length D) of sum-to-zero (orthogonal) contrasts with the intercept column *removed* (because I add the intercept, alpha, manually). D is the number of coefficients in the design matrix and N are the observations (rows).

In this model, this_alpha is not constrained to be between 0 and 5 even though alpha is. I would like this_alpha to also follow the constraint.

Some ideas:

min / maxing the value of this_alpha to fall within 0 and 5 would achieve what I'm after, but won't play nice with gradients, as the manual warns.
I thought maybe I could treat this_alpha as a linear predictor, passing it through the inv_logit function (multiplied by 5) to get a value bounded in the right range. I can see two problems with this: one, it adds an additional nonlinearity that makes the alpha and alpha_offsets parameters a bit hard to interpret and two, I'm not sure how to apply this to my other parameters (which have a lower bound of zero but no upper bounds – I suppose I could add some).
Fitting all parameters on a log scale would solve the "must be positive" constraint, but again would require more gymnastics on parameter interpretation. Ideally I could have interpretable parameters on alpha and its offsets.
Any suggestions on a good way to parameterise this model would be much appreciated.

Thanks

Tom

