Hi,

I read from the reference manual and previous posts in this group that the Poisson distribution does not support the declaration of a real-valued parameter. I converted a logistic growth JAGS model to STAN code as below. Lambda is calculated based on the parameters K,Q and b, which will cause it to be a real rather than an integer. This is then passed on to the Poisson distribution in the model block. I was wondering if there is any way to get around the integer declaration, please?

Any advice would be much appreciated!

Regards,
Cindy

#######################
###Model code for RSTAN
#######################

data {
int<lower=0> nobs;
int<lower=0> delta_D[nobs];
int<lower=0> delta_V[nobs];
int<lower=0> delta_W[nobs];
int<lower=0> delta_Z[nobs];
int<lower=0> T1[nobs];
int<lower=0> T2[nobs];
int<lower=0> pop;

}

parameters {
real alphaD;
real alphaV;
real alphaW;
real alphaZ;
real betaD;
real betaV;
real betaW;
real betaZ;

real muK;
real muQ;
real mub;

real<lower=0> sigmaK;
real<lower=0> sigmaQ;
real<lower=0> sigmab;

real thetaW;
real thetaK;
real thetaQ;
real thetab;

real <lower=0> I[nobs];

real<lower=0,upper=1> pD;
real<lower=0,upper=1> pV;
real<lower=0,upper=1> pW;
real<lower=0,upper=1> pZ;

}

transformed parameters {

real K;
real Q;
real b;
real lambda[nobs];
K<-exp(thetaK);
Q<-exp(thetaQ);
b<-exp(thetab);

for (j in 1:nobs)
{
lambda[j] <- 0.001 + (K/(Q*exp(-b*T2[j])+1))-(K/(Q*exp(-b*T1[j])+1));
}

}

model {

for (j in 1:nobs)
{
I[j] ~ poisson(pop*lambda[j]);

delta_D[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pD);

delta_V[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pV);

delta_Z[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pZ);

delta_W[j] ~ poisson(thetaW*pW*I[j]);
}

thetaK ~ dnorm(muK,sigmaK);
thetaQ ~ dnorm(muQ,sigmaQ);
thetab ~ dnorm(mub,sigmab);

pD ~ dbeta(alphaD,betaD);
pV ~ dbeta(alphaV,betaV);
pZ ~ dbeta(alphaZ,betaZ);
pW ~ dbeta(alphaW,betaW);

alphaD ~ dexp(0.001);
alphaV ~ dexp(0.001);
alphaZ ~ dexp(0.001);
alphaW ~ dexp(0.001);

betaD ~ dexp(0.001);
betaV ~ dexp(0.001);
betaZ ~ dexp(0.001);
betaW ~ dexp(0.001);

thetaW ~ dunif(0,1000000);

muK ~ dnorm(0,0.0001);
muQ ~ dnorm(0,0.0001);
mub ~ dnorm(0,0.0001);

sigmaK ~ dunif(0,100);
sigmaQ ~ dunif(0,100);
sigmab ~ dunif(0,100);

}

It

> On May 26, 2015, at 11:25 PM, Cindy Lim <cindy...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>
> Hi,
>
> I read from the reference manual and previous posts in this group that the Poisson distribution does not support the declaration of a real-valued parameter.

Real-valued outcome.  So in

  y ~ poisson(lambda);

the parameter lambda is real, but the outcome y is a count value (integer >= 0).

> I converted a logistic growth JAGS model to STAN code as below. Lambda is calculated based on the parameters K,Q and b, which will cause it to be a real rather than an integer. This is then passed on to the Poisson distribution in the model block. I was wondering if there is any way to get around the integer declaration, please?

So you can't do this:

  real <lower=0> I[nobs];

  I[j] ~ poisson(pop*lambda[j]);

because the outcome must be an integer.

That's just how the Poisson works.  You could replace the Poisson with
a positive-constrained real-valued density, but that's not going to plug
into the binomial.

I'd suggest looking into the negative binomial if you want to model
overdispersion.

- Bob

